:"A type’s own page lists only the members of its own module. Each page here collects the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"members that every other module adds to one type.","type":"text"}]}],"kind":"content"}],"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/Grafluent\/documentation\/Grafluent\/Extensions"},"references":{"doc://Trees/documentation/Trees/RootedTree":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Trees\/documentation\/Trees\/RootedTree","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"RootedTree"}],"abstract":[{"text":"A tree with a root: an undirected ","type":"text"},{"code":"Graph","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" with ","type":"text"},{"code":"root","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"parent(of:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"children(of:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"depth(of:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", and the queries tree algorithms start from. The vertices, edges and their","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"positions are the tree’s; the root orders ","type":"text"},{"code":"children(of:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" (each vertex’s incident edges in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"position order, without the edge to its parent) and so ","type":"text"},{"code":"preorder","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"RootedTree"}],"role":"symbol","title":"RootedTree","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/trees\/rootedtree"},"doc://Grafluent/documentation/Grafluent/Trees-Arborescence-Extensions":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Grafluent\/documentation\/Grafluent\/Trees-Arborescence-Extensions","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Members other modules add to "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Trees\/documentation\/Trees\/Arborescence"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"article","role":"collectionGroup","title":"Extensions to Arborescence","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/grafluent\/trees-arborescence-extensions"},"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/Hypergraph":{"kind":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"Hypergraph"}],"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"protocol"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"Hypergraph"}],"title":"Hypergraph","type":"topic","abstract":[{"text":"An undirected hypergraph: a finite set of vertices and a finite collection of hyperedges, each a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"set of vertices of any size, the empty set included (Berge, ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Hypergraphs"}]},{"text":", 1989). Not a ","type":"text"},{"code":"Graph","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":",","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"and no shared parent with one: a hyperedge is not an edge. The ","type":"text"},{"code":"Hypergraphs","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" module’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"incidenceGraphView","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" reads a hypergraph as its bipartite incidence graph (the Levi graph), which is","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"how the graph algorithms reach it.","type":"text"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Hypergraph","role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/graphprotocols\/hypergraph"},"doc://Grafluent/documentation/Grafluent/GraphProtocols-Graph-Extensions":{"url":"\/documentation\/grafluent\/graphprotocols-graph-extensions","abstract":[{"text":"Members other modules add to ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"kind":"article","role":"collectionGroup","title":"Extensions to Graph","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/Grafluent\/documentation\/Grafluent\/GraphProtocols-Graph-Extensions"},"doc://Grafluent/documentation/Grafluent/GraphProtocols-DirectedHypergraph-Extensions":{"kind":"article","title":"Extensions to DirectedHypergraph","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/Grafluent\/documentation\/Grafluent\/GraphProtocols-DirectedHypergraph-Extensions","abstract":[{"text":"Members other modules add to ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedHypergraph"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"role":"collectionGroup","url":"\/documentation\/grafluent\/graphprotocols-directedhypergraph-extensions"},"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/MutableDirectedGraph":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/MutableDirectedGraph","fragments":[{"text":"protocol","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MutableDirectedGraph","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"text":"A bidirectional directed graph that generic code can mutate: Boost’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"MutableGraph"},{"text":" concept","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"(","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"add_vertex"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"add_edge"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"remove_edge"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"remove_vertex"},{"text":"), for a directed representation. It exists so that a wrapper which adds a rule, such as ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DirectedAcyclicGraph"},{"text":",","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"can own any of the mutable representations as its base and still run at the base’s speed.","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MutableDirectedGraph","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol","title":"MutableDirectedGraph","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/graphprotocols\/mutabledirectedgraph"},"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/DirectedHypergraph":{"kind":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"DirectedHypergraph","kind":"identifier"}],"fragments":[{"text":"protocol","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"DirectedHypergraph","kind":"identifier"}],"title":"DirectedHypergraph","type":"topic","abstract":[{"text":"A directed hypergraph: a finite set of vertices and a finite collection of hyperedges, each an","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"ordered pair of vertex sets, its sources (tail) and its targets (head). Gallo, Longo, Pallottino","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"and Nguyen, “Directed hypergraphs and applications”, ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Discrete Applied Mathematics"}]},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"42(2–3):177–201, 1993, define a hyperarc as “an ordered pair … of (possibly empty) disjoint","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"subsets”; here either side may be empty and a vertex may be on both, the hypergraph form of a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"self-loop (XGI’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"DiHypergraph","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" allows both).","type":"text"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedHypergraph","role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/graphprotocols\/directedhypergraph"},"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/BidirectionalDirectedGraph":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/BidirectionalDirectedGraph","fragments":[{"text":"protocol","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"BidirectionalDirectedGraph","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A directed graph that also answers in-neighborhoods: Boost’s "},{"code":"BidirectionalGraph","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":". Kept apart"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"from "},{"code":"DirectedGraph"